  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nA weak shortwave is passing through the region today, low to mid \nlevel clouds remain with some lingering snow showers primarily on \nthe ID/WY border. In its wake, conditions will begin to quiet down \nuntil the holiday, and temperatures will remain seasonal as an upper-\nlevel ridge sets in. Beginning Thursday night, the pattern will \nbegin to become unsettled as another wave of moisture begins to move \nin. Our region will be under a NW flow regime, leading to increased \nsnow chances for the central mountains and eastern highlands. \namounts generally look light with current guidance suggesting a few \ninches. Snow levels look to remain above 6000ft. QPF amounts for \nvalley locations look limited, especially in the northern Snake \nPlain where shadowing is possible. The low will eventually progress \ninland by the end of the weekend bringing a more appreciable chance \nfor widespread precipitation to the region. Probabilistic \nguidance from The NBM over the weekend into early next week \nsuggests 25th to 75th percentile QPF values ranging from 0.1 to \n0.5 inches for valley locations, with around 0.25\" being the \nmean. The higher elevation QPF values are much more aggressive \nin the 1-2 inch range. Snow levels will be decreasing late \nweekend into early next week as well, increasing the chance for \nrain/snow in valley locations.",
